# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# desktop shell
/desktop/node_modules/
/desktop/dist/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...and the template is opted in. It holds no secrets — only the variable
# names and what each provider is for — and it is the only place that documents
# which sources are keyless. Caught by the glob above, it was never tracked, so
# a clone got no template at all.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Superseded review reports, left on disk on purpose.
# Blind-review rounds against 1.7.0 / d2d2572 — twenty-one releases stale, and
# every finding in them is either fixed or restated in the 1.28.0 audit. Their
# siblings from the same batch (W1/W2/W3-REPORT.md, W5-BUILD-NOTES.md) were
# committed at the time and stay tracked; these never were. Ignored rather than
# deleted so the history is still readable, and so `git status` is quiet for
# someone returning to this repo cold.
W*-ROUND*-REPORT.md
WHOLE-*-REPORT.md

# Impeccable's hook cache is per-machine scratch. config.json is NOT ignored:
# it carries the reasoned suppression for the NOVA wordmark, which is a
# decision, not a cache, and re-deciding it every session wastes the argument.
.impeccable/hook.cache.json
